---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
import MoodTile from '../components/MoodTile.astro';
import { CONDITION_INFO, DIMENSION_INFO, STANCE_INFO } from '../lib/labels';
import { CONDITIONS, DIMENSIONS } from '../lib/schema';
import { STANCES } from '../lib/stance';
import { READING } from '../lib/ui';
import { REPO_URL, SITE_NAME } from '../site.config';

const h2 = 'casual mt-14 mb-3 text-2xl font-bold';
const p = 'mt-4';
---

<Layout
  title={`How the ratings work | ${SITE_NAME}`}
  description="How Are We AI Friendly? rates open source AI policies: the stances, what each area covers, and how sources are checked."
>
  <article class:list={[READING, 'pt-12 sm:pt-20']}>
    <h1 class="casual text-4xl leading-tight font-extrabold tracking-tight sm:text-5xl">How the ratings work</h1>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg text-muted">
      More and more open source projects publish rules about AI: some welcome it, some ask you to say when you
      used it, some ban it outright. This site gathers those rules in one place so you can check before you open
      that pull request.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>How friendly is friendly?</h2>
    <p>
      Each project gets one rating for how friendly it is towards AI-assisted contributions. The rating comes from
      the project’s rules for code, with bans on AI-written issues or text pulling it down.
    </p>
    <dl class="my-4 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
      {
        STANCES.map((s) => (
          <div class="flex gap-4 py-4">
            <MoodTile stance={s} decorative />
            <div>
              <dt>
                <strong class="font-bold">{STANCE_INFO[s].label}</strong>
                <span class="text-muted"> ({STANCE_INFO[s].plain})</span>
              </dt>
              <dd class="mt-0.5">{STANCE_INFO[s].tagline} <span class="text-muted">{STANCE_INFO[s].meaning}</span></dd>
            </div>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>

    <h2 class={h2}>What we look at</h2>
    <dl class="my-4 divide-y divide-line border-y border-line">
      {
        DIMENSIONS.map((d) => (
          <div class="py-3.5">
            <dt class="flex items-center gap-2 font-semibold">
              <Icon name={DIMENSION_INFO[d].icon} size={15} class="text-faint" />
              {DIMENSION_INFO[d].label}
            </dt>
            <dd class="mt-1.5 pl-[23px] text-muted">{DIMENSION_INFO[d].meaning}</dd>
          </div>
        ))
      }
    </dl>
    <p class={p}>
      Each area gets its own face on the same scale. Some come with conditions:
    </p>
    <ul class="my-4 grid gap-2">
      {
        CONDITIONS.map((c) => (
          <li class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline gap-x-2.5 gap-y-0.5 text-muted sm:flex-nowrap">
            <Icon name={CONDITION_INFO[c].icon} size={14} class="self-center text-faint" />
            <strong class="font-semibold text-ink sm:min-w-34">{CONDITION_INFO[c].short}</strong>
            {CONDITION_INFO[c].label}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class={p}>
      Conditions that every good contribution meets anyway, like taking responsibility for your work, keep an area
      <em>Friendly</em>. Extra hoops, like mandatory disclosure, make it <em>Mostly friendly</em>. Allowing only
      minor help makes it <em>Unfriendly</em>.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Where the data comes from</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li>Someone points us at a project’s policy: a <code>CONTRIBUTING</code> file, a code of conduct, a docs page.</li>
      <li>We save a plain-text snapshot of each source in the repository, so every change is visible in git.</li>
      <li>
        An AI model reads the full text and fills in the ratings. Each rating must come with a quote, and a script
        checks that every quote appears word for word in the snapshot.
      </li>
      <li>A human reviews every change before it goes live.</li>
      <li>Every week the sources are fetched again. If the text changed, the project is re-checked and reviewed.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class={p}>
      Yes, we use AI to track AI policies. We think that’s fine, as long as a human signs off on every change.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2} id="add">Add or fix a project</h2>
    <p>
      <a href={`${REPO_URL}/issues/new?template=add-project.yml`}>Open an “Add a project” issue</a> with links to
      the project’s policy. Spotted a mistake? Use the “Suggest a correction” link on any project page.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>For AI agents</h2>
    <p>
      Machine-readable versions of everything live at <a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a>,{' '}
      <a href="/llms-full.txt"><code>/llms-full.txt</code></a> and <a href="/projects.json"><code>/projects.json</code></a>.
      Every project page also has a Markdown twin at <code>/projects/&lt;slug&gt;.md</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 class={h2}>Related work</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-1 pl-5">
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/melissawm/open-source-ai-contribution-policies">open-source-ai-contribution-policies</a>,
        a community list of AI policies.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://redmonk.com/kholterhoff/2026/02/26/generative-ai-policy-landscape-in-open-source/">The Generative AI Policy Landscape in Open Source</a>{' '}
        by Kate Holterhoff at RedMonk.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="mt-14 border-t border-line pt-5 text-sm text-muted">
      This is a summary, not legal advice. Projects change their rules and individual maintainers may be stricter
      than the written policy, so read the source before you contribute.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
